2 billion of oil a controversial move by  the democratic republic of congo drc is  a new destination of investments for oil  and gas companies it's auctioning off  oil and gas development rights to 30  blocks of peatland tropical forest in  the congo basin welcome to drc  the president insists this does not mean  his government is backtracking on  commitments made at last year's un  climate summit  [Music]  i would like to reassure our partners of  our determination to carry out  exploration using modern technology that  protects the environment the fauna and  the flora and preserves the ecosystems  and ecological balance  the congo basin covers 530 million  hectares in central africa and is home  to seventy percent of the continent's  forests  some of the blocks the dr congo plan to  develop are located on lake kivu  lake tanganyika one is in a coastal  region alongside the albertin graben in  the east african rift valley  other plots are in the virunga national  park home to protected mountain gorillas  and other endangered species  environmentalists and climate activists  warn drilling in these areas could be  catastrophic we're talking about a very  sensitive ecosystem calling peatland  that is storing more than 30 billion ton  of carbon and that is about three years  of emissional year over mission of the  entire planet that means any oil  exploitation in this area would be a  carbon bomb that it really exposed and  the humanity won't be the same anymore  those in favor of drilling say fossil  fuel production would boost development  in one of the world's poorest countries  this is a moment in which we're trying  to promote our resources the democratic  republic of congo is a potentially rich  country but at the moment it remains  just potential the government estimates  there are 22 billion barrels of crude  reserves and is targeting production of  200 000 barrels per day but critics say  there's no guarantee the wealth will  benefit the congolese people haru 